# ScopeForge

Next.js 16, React 19, and strict TypeScript. Sample briefs live in `data/briefs/` and curated scopes in `data/mock-scopes/` (Northwind Logistics, Apex BioLabs, Helix Pharma, Sterling FinTech). The effort model is `data/effort-model.json`. The public app is https://scopeforge-sable.vercel.app and runs without provider keys.

## Synthetic data only

Synthetic data only. Never add real company, customer, or personal data. No employer names or work data of any kind. Briefs in `data/briefs/` stay fictional, start with "SYNTHETIC DEMO DATA. Fictional company. Not real.", and use `.example` addresses. Scopes in `data/mock-scopes/` stay fictional.

## No secrets in code

No secrets in code. Do not commit real keys. `.env.example` holds placeholders only (`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `LLM_PROVIDER`, `LLM_MODEL`, `FORGE_ACCESS_TOKEN`). Never use a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` key. Keep `.env*` gitignored.

## Tests before a commit or pull request

These must pass before any commit or pull request. They are the checks in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`:

- `npm run lint` — `eslint .`
- `npm run typecheck` — `tsc --noEmit`
- `npm run test` — Vitest (`vitest run`)
- `npm run build` — Next.js production build
- `npm run test:e2e` — Playwright (`playwright test`). Install the browser with `npx playwright install --with-deps chromium` when it is missing.

## README

The README follows this order: problem, who it's for, demo GIF, live demo link (https://scopeforge-sable.vercel.app), architecture diagram, eval results, cost and latency, limits and next steps. Only real measured numbers. Never invent metrics, eval scores, cost, or latency.

## Demo mode

Demo mode only. No paid AI calls. Never set provider keys on the deployed project. Production never makes live model calls. Evals use built-in or recorded outputs only.
